import json
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'dist'
# Original teaching notes. Edges are pedagogical choices, not extracted deductions.
rows=[
('logic','Statements and implication','A statement has a truth value. An implication says that whenever P holds, Q holds.','To prove P implies Q, assume P and justify Q. The reverse implication requires its own argument.','If n is divisible by 4, then n is even: n=4k=2(2k).','',[]),
('sets','Sets and membership','A set collects objects; membership says an object belongs to that collection.','Two sets are equal when they have exactly the same members. Prove both inclusions.','A ∩ B = B ∩ A. An object belongs to either side exactly when it belongs to both A and B.','', ['logic']),
('functions','Functions','A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain.','Injective means distinct inputs have distinct outputs. Surjective means every target is reached.','The function f(x)=2x on the real numbers is injective: 2a=2b implies a=b.','',['sets']),
('contradiction','Proof by contradiction','Assume the negation of your claim and derive something impossible.','State the negation precisely, then identify the incompatible conclusions.','There is no largest integer. If N were largest, the integer N+1 would be larger.','',['logic']),
('induction','Mathematical induction','Prove a starting case and a rule that advances one case to the next.','Induction on positive integers needs both the base case and the implication from n to n+1.','1+…+n=n(n+1)/2. Base n=1 holds. Adding n+1 to the formula gives (n+1)(n+2)/2, the next case.','',['logic']),
('integers','Integers and divisibility','Integers are whole numbers; a divides b when b is an integer multiple of a.','Divisibility replaces numerical size with multiplicative structure.','If a divides b and b divides c, write b=ak and c=bℓ. Then c=a(kℓ), so a divides c.','',['sets']),
('rational','Rational numbers','A rational number is a ratio of two integers with nonzero denominator.','Different fractions can represent the same number. Arithmetic must preserve nonzero denominators.','The sum of a/b and c/d is (ad+bc)/(bd). The numerator is an integer and bd is nonzero, so the sum is rational.','',['integers']),
('prime','Prime numbers','A prime is an integer greater than one whose only positive divisors are one and itself.','Prime factorization turns multiplication into information about prime factors.','There are infinitely many primes. If p₁,…,pₖ were all primes, N=p₁…pₖ+1 has a prime divisor. None of the listed primes divides N, a contradiction. Here we use that every integer greater than one has a prime divisor.','',['integers','contradiction']),
('modular','Modular arithmetic','Two integers are congruent modulo m when their difference is divisible by m.','Fix a positive modulus m. Congruence records remainders rather than full integers.','If a≡b and c≡d mod m, then a+c≡b+d: (a+c)−(b+d)=(a−b)+(c−d), a sum of multiples of m.','',['integers']),
('counting','Counting and bijections','Count a collection by matching it with another collection of known size.','A bijection is an invertible function and preserves the size of finite sets.','A set with n elements has 2ⁿ subsets: each element has two independent choices, included or excluded.','',['functions','induction']),
('graphs','Graphs and paths','A graph consists of vertices and edges; a path follows edges between vertices.','Graph properties describe connections independently of how a picture is drawn.','In a finite undirected graph without loops, the sum of degrees equals twice the edge count: each edge contributes once at each endpoint.','',['sets','counting']),
('inequalities','Inequalities and bounds','Bounds tell us how large or small a quantity can be without finding it exactly.','State the domain and sign conditions before multiplying or dividing inequalities.','For real a,b, (a−b)²≥0 gives a²+b²≥2ab. Equality holds exactly when a=b.','',['rational']),
('sequences','Sequences','A sequence is a function whose inputs are positive integers.','Think of aₙ as the value at stage n. A pattern in the first few terms is not a proof about all terms.','The sequence aₙ=1/n is decreasing because n+1>n>0 implies 1/(n+1)<1/n.','',['functions','inequalities']),
('limits','Limits and convergence','A sequence converges when its terms eventually stay arbitrarily close to a fixed value.','For every ε>0 there must be an N such that n≥N implies |aₙ−L|<ε.','1/n tends to zero: given ε>0 choose an integer N>1/ε. For n≥N, 0<1/n≤1/N<ε.','',['sequences']),
('continuity','Continuity','Small changes in input produce small changes in output, at the point under consideration.','At a, for every ε>0 choose δ>0 so |x−a|<δ implies |f(x)−f(a)|<ε.','f(x)=2x is continuous: choose δ=ε/2. Then |f(x)−f(a)|=2|x−a|<ε.','',['limits','functions']),
('derivative','Derivatives','A derivative measures the limiting rate of change at a point.','The derivative is the limit of (f(x+h)−f(x))/h as nonzero h tends to zero, when it exists.','For f(x)=x², the quotient is 2x+h. Its limit as h tends to zero is 2x.','',['limits']),
('integral','Integrals','Integration adds contributions over a region, often through limits of finite sums.','For Riemann integration on an interval, refine partitions and compare upper and lower sums.','For a constant c on [a,b], every Riemann sum is c times the sum of interval widths, hence c(b−a).','',['limits','counting']),
('vectors','Vectors and linear combinations','Vectors can be added and scaled; linear combinations mix these operations.','A vector space must obey the vector space axioms over a specified field.','The span of u and v is closed under addition: (au+bv)+(cu+dv)=(a+c)u+(b+d)v.','',['rational','sets']),
('linear','Linear maps','A linear map preserves vector addition and scalar multiplication.','Matrices represent linear maps after bases are chosen.','The kernel is closed under addition: if T(u)=T(v)=0, then T(u+v)=T(u)+T(v)=0. It also contains zero and is closed under scaling, so it is a subspace.','',['vectors','functions']),
('norm','Norms and distances','A norm measures vector length; a metric measures distance between two points.','A metric must be nonnegative, symmetric, zero only on equal points, and satisfy the triangle inequality.','Given a norm, d(x,y)=‖x−y‖ satisfies the triangle inequality because x−z=(x−y)+(y−z). The other metric axioms follow from the norm axioms.','',['vectors','inequalities']),
('groups','Groups and symmetry','A group combines elements associatively, with an identity and an inverse for every element.','Symmetries form groups under composition. Group operations need not commute.','An identity is unique: if e and f are both identities, then ef=f because e is an identity, and ef=e because f is one. Thus e=f.','',['functions','sets']),
('rings','Rings and polynomials','Rings support addition and multiplication; polynomials encode finite algebraic expressions.','The exact ring axioms, including whether an identity is required, must be stated.','In any ring, a·0=0: a·0=a(0+0)=a·0+a·0. Subtract a·0 in the additive group.','',['groups']),
('topology','Open sets and topology','Topology describes neighborhoods and continuity without requiring a numerical distance.','A topology contains the empty and whole sets, is closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.','An arbitrary union of open metric balls is open: each point belongs to one ball and has a sufficiently small ball around it contained in that ball. The triangle inequality justifies the containment.','',['sets','norm','continuity']),
('compact','Compactness','Compactness means every open cover has a finite subcover.','Compactness can turn local information into a finite global argument. It does not mean finite cardinality.','Every finite set is compact: for each of its finitely many points choose one member of an open cover containing it. Those finitely many members cover the set.','',['topology','counting']),
('measure','Measure and probability','A measure assigns sizes to sets, adding over disjoint countable unions.','A probability measure has total mass one. Specify the measurable sets before assigning probabilities.','P(Aᶜ)=1−P(A): A and its complement are disjoint and their union is the whole sample space, so additivity gives P(A)+P(Aᶜ)=1.','',['sets','limits']),
('expectation','Expectation','Expectation is the average of a random variable weighted by probabilities.','For finite random variables, compute a sum of value times probability. Infinite cases need integrability conditions.','For finite random variables, E[X+Y]=Σω(X(ω)+Y(ω))P(ω)=E[X]+E[Y] by distributing a finite sum. No independence is needed.','',['measure','integral']),
('algorithm','Algorithms and invariants','An algorithm gives explicit steps; an invariant is a property maintained by those steps.','Correctness needs a maintained invariant, a conclusion on termination, and a termination argument.','Euclid’s algorithm preserves common divisors because a=qb+r implies d divides a and b exactly when d divides b and r. Nonnegative remainders strictly decrease until zero, so it terminates.','',['integers','induction']),
('convex','Convexity','A convex set contains the segment joining any two of its points.','For x,y in C and 0≤t≤1, require tx+(1−t)y to belong to C.','The intersection of convex sets is convex: a segment between two points of the intersection lies in each set, hence in their intersection.','',['vectors','sets']),
('differential','Differential equations','A differential equation constrains an unknown function through its derivatives.','Solving includes specifying a domain and checking any initial or boundary conditions.','For y′=y and y(0)=1, y=eˣ is a solution because its derivative is itself and e⁰=1. This verifies the candidate; uniqueness needs a separate argument.','',['derivative','functions']),
('dynamics','Iteration and dynamical systems','Iterate a function repeatedly to study how states evolve.','A fixed point satisfies f(x)=x. Other points may approach it, cycle, or behave differently.','For xₙ₊₁=xₙ/2, induction gives xₙ=x₀/2ⁿ. Thus every real starting value tends to the fixed point zero.','',['functions','induction','limits'])]
subjects={
'Theoretical computer science':['algorithm','graphs'], 'Combinatorics':['counting','graphs'], 'Algebraic and complex geometry':['rings','topology'], 'Number theory':['prime','modular','rational'], 'Probability and statistical mechanics':['measure','expectation'], 'Differential geometry':['derivative','topology','linear'], 'Mathematical physics':['differential','linear'], 'Operator algebras':['linear','rings','norm'], 'Algebra':['groups','rings'], 'Topology':['topology','compact'], 'Real and complex analysis':['limits','continuity','integral'], 'Partial differential equations':['differential','integral'], 'Convex and metric geometry':['convex','norm'], 'Group theory':['groups'], 'Dynamical systems and ergodic theory':['dynamics','measure'], 'Functional analysis':['norm','linear','compact'], 'Mathematical logic':['logic','sets','induction']}
nodes=[];edges=[]
for id,title,summary,detail,proof,url,parents in rows:
 nodes.append(dict(id='concept:'+id,kind='Concept',title=title,summary=summary,detail=detail,proof=proof,url=url,extraction='Curated learning note and elementary example; not a formal verification or a claim about a manuscript’s dependencies.'))
 for parent in parents:edges.append(dict(source='concept:'+id,target='concept:'+parent,type='LEARNING_PREREQUISITE',evidence='Curated teaching sequence; not a logical necessity.'))
for n in json.loads((root/'catalogue.json').read_text())['nodes']:
 if n['kind']=='Family':
  for concept in subjects[n['subject']]:edges.append(dict(source=n['id'],target='concept:'+concept,type='SUGGESTED_BACKGROUND',evidence='Suggested from subject classification only; not extracted from this proof.'))
for n in nodes:n['uses']=sum(e['target']==n['id'] for e in edges)
(root/'foundations.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(nodes=nodes,edges=edges),ensure_ascii=False))
ids={n['id'] for n in nodes}; assert len(ids)==len(nodes)
def visit(id,path):
 assert id not in path, path+[id]
 for e in edges:
  if e['source']==id:visit(e['target'],path+[id])
for id in ids:visit(id,[])
assert all(e['target'] in ids for e in edges)
print(len(nodes),'concepts;',len(edges),'learning connections; prerequisite graph acyclic')
